[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108290,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ossifying fibromas (OFs) are a distinct group of fibro-osseous benign neoplasms composed of woven bone maturing to dense bone and/or cementum-like masses within a hypercellular stroma of spindle cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108291,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "<p>9262/0 Ossifying fibroma</p>\r\n<p>9262/0 Juvenile trabecular ossifying fibroma</p>\r\n<p>9262/0 Psammomatoid ossifying fibroma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T14:25:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108292,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E83.1 &amp; XH6M86 Benign osteogenic tumours of bone or articular cartilage of lower jaw &amp; Ossifying fibroma</p>\r\n<p>2E83.0 &amp; XH6M86 Benign osteogenic tumours of bone or articular cartilage of skull or face &amp; Ossifying fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108293,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> cemento-ossifying fibroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> cementifying fibroma; periodontoma; juvenile active ossifying fibroma; juvenile aggressive ossifying fibroma; juvenile ossifying fibroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108294,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "<p>Juvenile trabecular ossifying fibroma (JTOF); psammomatoid ossifying fibroma (PsOF)</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:47:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108295,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "<p>OF and JTOF affect the jaws {31285096; 20819127}, with OF usually arising in the mandible and JTOF in the maxilla. PsOF affects facial bones and sinuses more frequently than jaws {8630892; 11925539}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:48:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108296,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "<p>All types manifest as asymptomatic bony swellings, limited by the periosteum, that may reach a large size, with disfigurement, displacement, and resorption of tooth roots. Some tumours may show rapid clinical growth {31285096; 11925539; 8630892}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>OF initially appears as a well-defined corticated radiolucency, maturing to a mixed radiolucency or sometimes to a dense radiopacity with a thin radiolucent rim; the juvenile types are less opaque {20026707}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:48:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108297,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "<p>OF is the most common primary non-odontogenic neoplasm of the jaws {9146865; 20819127; 32114009}. The incidence of PsOF peaks in the second and third decades of life, and JTOF affects children aged 2&ndash;12&nbsp;years {11925539; 25409854; 8630892; 20819127}. OF has a strong female predilection. Classic OF is rare in the paediatric population unless syndromic.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:49:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108298,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108299,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "<p>Multiple OFs are present in hyperparathyroidism&ndash;jaw tumour syndrome, caused by a germline mutation of <em>CDC73</em>, a mutation also identified in sporadic tumours {31929790; 16458039}. JTOF and PsOF show rearrangements involving <em>RASAL1</em> and <em>MDM2</em>, and they may show <em>MDM2</em> amplification but not MDM2 overexpression by immunohistochemistry {24925056}. Despite the histological similarity between OF and fibrous dysplasia, <em>GNAS</em> (<em>GNAS1</em>) mutations are not seen in OF.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:49:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108300,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>OF is a soft, gritty, or hard tumour with a well-defined margin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108301,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "<p>OF has woven bone maturing to dense cementum-like masses that coalesce centrally and have a cellular rim, lacking nuclear atypia and with occasional mitoses. JTOF is unencapsulated but well defined, with prominent long and slender osteoid seams (paint-brush strokes), woven bone, and a hypercellular stroma of spindle cells and fibroblasts {11925539}. Mineralization is minimal and irregular, sometimes mimicking osteosarcoma. Mitoses are few. PsOF has a similar periphery but has small, rounded, non-coalescing, densely mineralized ossicles with an unmineralized, eosinophilic rim {8630892}. Secondary aneurysmal bone cyst or a giant cell reaction may develop {11925539}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-04T17:49:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108302,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 348,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:07:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108303,
